Fragrance Rater database migrations (Alembic, async SQLAlchemy, PostgreSQL 16)
==============================================================================

ROLLBACK IS RESTORE, NOT DOWNGRADE
----------------------------------
Four revisions refuse to downgrade and raise RuntimeError, because reversing
them would destroy encounter, outcome, or frozen-prediction history:

    c731b42e9a01  controlled calibration
    e4b1c2d3f4a5  recommendation outcome measurement
    9ded7f54996c  typed calibration observations (retires the responses blob)
    72fe56efd128  prediction_snapshots

4edb5c967539 (R8 PR 2) downgrades for real (drops its triggers and views,
converts jsonb back to json, restores the now() defaults) but refuses while
any observation carries an idempotency key.

Nothing can be downgraded past c731b42e9a01. To roll a production database
back, restore a verified pre-upgrade backup (docs/deployment/
p1-release-readiness.md). tests/unit/test_models/test_naming_convention.py
asserts all four raises. Several other revisions raise only when the data
would make the downgrade lossy; their messages name the conflicting rows.

Production runs `alembic upgrade head` on container start
(docker-entrypoint.sh), so a merged migration reaches production on the next
deploy. Take a backup first.

POSTGRESQL ONLY
---------------
The chain cannot replay on SQLite: 5d2e7c9f9c92 uses gen_random_uuid() and a
non-batch primary-key drop, and 2c8c3971bfd8 uses RENAME CONSTRAINT. That is
why the SQLite migration tests under tests/integration/ build their
pre-migration state from Base.metadata.create_all() and hand-revert it;
those tests check one migration's logic, not drift.

Drift is checked against real PostgreSQL by
tests/integration/test_schema_parity_postgres.py, which CI's
postgres-integration job runs after `alembic upgrade head`. It asserts:

  * compare_metadata(..., compare_type, compare_server_default) == []
    (what `alembic check` and autogenerate see), and
  * every constraint and index name in the database equals the name the
    models give it. compare_metadata ignores CHECK constraints and
    primary/foreign-key names, so without this a dropped or misnamed CHECK
    would go unnoticed.
  * the head revision downgrades and re-upgrades back to parity.

To run them locally, point P1_DATABASE_URL at a scratch PostgreSQL 16
database you have upgraded to head:

    DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://... uv run alembic upgrade head
    P1_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://... \
        uv run pytest tests/integration -m integration --no-cov

NAMING CONVENTION
-----------------
Base.metadata carries fragrance_rater.core.database.NAMING_CONVENTION:

    pk_<table>                       ix_<table>_<column>
    fk_<table>_<column>              uq_<table>_<col1>_<col2>...
    ck_<table>_<rule>

Every CheckConstraint in a model needs a short name= giving only the rule
(name="liking_range"); the convention adds the ck_<table>_ prefix. A few
older objects keep explicit names (uq_fragrance_version, uq_reviewer_name,
uq_fragrance_note_position, ix_fragrance_search); keep using them as they
are. In a migration, refer to constraints by their convention name, and give
every new constraint one, so later migrations and SQLite batch mode can
address it.

env.py applies the models' metadata, and with it the convention, only to
`revision --autogenerate` and `check`. Upgrade, downgrade, and stamp run with
no target metadata, so the migrations written before the convention keep
producing the names production has (5d2e7c9f9c92 drops fragrance_notes_pkey by
name). The consequence: a new migration's unnamed constraint gets a
PostgreSQL-invented name, and the name-parity test fails. Autogenerate already
renders convention names as op.f("..."); keep them.

VIEWS
-----
620c8c861268 creates v_training_rows (R8, X-05) over evaluations,
calibration_observations, calibration_presentations, calibration_memberships,
calibration_sessions, calibration_enrollments, and fragrances. 4edb5c967539
(R8 PR 2) redefines it (adds `revealed` and the latest-answer rule) and adds
v_manager_linkage (X-06) over the same calibration tables. The models do
not declare either, so autogenerate and `alembic check` never see them.
PostgreSQL refuses to drop or retype a column a view reads, and SQLite batch
mode cannot rebuild a table a view names, so a later migration that alters
one of those columns drops both views first (`DROP VIEW IF EXISTS
v_manager_linkage`, then `v_training_rows`) and recreates them last. The
newest definition is the one the unit suite builds: tests/conftest.py runs
4edb5c967539's V_TRAINING_ROWS_SQL and V_MANAGER_LINKAGE_SQL after
create_all(); move that pointer when a later migration redefines a view.

TRIGGERS
--------
4edb5c967539 adds BEFORE triggers on PostgreSQL only (D-08, Q14): append-only
calibration_observations (only training_eligibility may change), frozen
calibration_checkpoints, calibration_checkpoint_predictions and
feature_snapshots, frozen prediction_snapshots (only the outcome link may be
set, once), immutable memberships of an active program, and immutable
identity columns of a fragrance any membership references. They raise
SQLSTATE 23001 (an IntegrityError). A test that needs to clean up rows in
those tables must run inside a transaction it rolls back (see
tests/integration/test_r8_hardening_postgres.py), not DELETE them.

WRITING A MIGRATION
-------------------
1. Always create the file with `uv run alembic revision -m "..."` (add
   --autogenerate against a database at head to get a draft). Never
   hand-type a revision id: two PRs once picked the same one
   (tests/integration/test_migration_graph_integrity.py now catches it).
   Then run `uv run ruff check --fix` and `uv run ruff format` on the new
   file: an empty revision's unused imports and `pass` bodies are the only
   lint findings script.py.mako leaves.
2. Review every autogenerated operation. env.py sets compare_type,
   compare_server_default, and render_as_batch, so the draft should contain
   only your change; if it proposes anything else, the database or the
   models have drifted. Stop and find out why.
3. Wrap ALTERs in op.batch_alter_table(...) so the per-migration SQLite
   tests can run them. Batch mode is a plain ALTER TABLE on PostgreSQL.
4. Write literal names and values in the migration. Do not import models or
   derive names from current metadata; a migration must mean the same thing
   after the models change.
5. Write a real downgrade(), or raise RuntimeError naming the backup to
   restore if reversing it would lose data, and add the revision to the
   list above and to RESTORE_ONLY_REVISIONS in the naming-convention test.
6. Before pushing, run `uv run alembic upgrade head` and the parity tests
   against a scratch PostgreSQL 16, then `uv run alembic check`. It must
   print "No new upgrade operations detected."
